{"kind":"assert","text":"Hugh: include merge succession effects when preparing a review request.\n\nThe approved notifier head #20008 provides a concrete case. Its 24 reviewed artifacts were sufficient for the implementation review, but the subsequent merge plan refused to retire an older internal/service directory artifact outside those reviewed paths. Deleting the old generated JavaScript bundle also changes its covering directory. A cleanup request then allowed the author to retire the two old bundle artifacts, but the directory retirement stopped again: three pages still cite it. I independently checked the candidate deletion and those three citations. The exact events and commands are in the [case evidence](review-succession-preflight-evidence.json). This is one observed landing, not a measured project-wide delay.\n\nThese refusals preserve the current contract. [ValidateReach](internal/mergeplan/mergeplan.go@07753351d69c05dedec58f4e2561a793054312a5:855) bounds cross-author retirement by reviewed paths. The [succession rules](docs/reference/gs/merge.md@07753351d69c05dedec58f4e2561a793054312a5:498) explicitly include covering directories when a file is deleted or renamed. A narrower server.go artifact does not replace the directory at the same exact path. Both linked files are byte-identical to current main dfbf4089.\n\nRecommendation: extend the single review-request preparation proposed in #19969 with the existing succession planner's read-only result. Before filing, show the selected candidate and target heads, added/changed/deleted paths, covering-directory effects, required successor paths, and any planned retirement outside the selected review scope. For a retirement requiring its author's action, also identify current citation or live-commitment constraints using the existing checks. Show the smallest actionable correction: include an appropriate same-head directory artifact in the intended review, or commission the necessary source/citation repair. Do not silently widen the review or assume every matching artifact belongs in it.\n\nThis should be one preparation result shared by review authoring and merge planning, with no second succession algorithm or new lifecycle state. It must work before an approval exists; manufacturing a temporary approval merely to run preflight would defeat the purpose. The result is advisory at its recorded frontier, not advance authority. Actual review and merge still recheck current facts. An ordinary modification continues to carry wider pointers; it must not acquire the deletion rule by accident.\n\nA small acceptance set would cover this generated-bundle replacement, an ordinary modification with a carried directory, deletion under another author's reviewed and unreviewed directory, a still-cited predecessor, an independently protected sibling, and target/frontier movement after preparation. The useful measure is late succession refusals that force another handoff or review after approval. This supplements #19969's malformed-request prevention; it is a recommendation for your review, not an adopted implementation task. The current #20430 remains owned by Codex and Claude. Its resolution must keep the existing candidate chain live until the replacement review is complete.","body":{"head":"07753351d69c05dedec58f4e2561a793054312a5","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:765878117cb1813fae8925e4dd84dc5955ec94f9, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9704e040cdf1306e1eddfb4f9d647dd84189efc2, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b098c92f86316b8d042cfba8f8e2d177850738dc, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b4380583e7a75f86ceb74eb71aab4a550fa54715, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b7306c1482098773bdbcdab842ce8af077168e6c stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:001b2e3b06105d22946682cb90201bc66c1c6a26, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e